"CSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn1188view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/\",\"pattern\":\"abort\"}); Total lines: 2053\nL198: ## 3. Tokenizing and Parsing CSS\nL199: \nL200: User agents must use the parsing rules described in this specification to generate the cite134†[CSSOM] trees from text/css resources. Together, these rules define what is referred to as the CSS parser.\nL201: This specification defines the parsing rules for CSS documents, whether they are syntactically correct or not. Certain points in the parsing algorithm are said to be parse errors. The error handling for parse errors is well-defined: user agents must either act as described below when encountering such problems, or must abort processing at the first error that they encounter for which they do not wish to apply the rules described below.\nL202: Conformance checkers must report at least one parse error condition to the user if one or more parse error conditions exist in the document and must not report parse error conditions if none exist in the document. Conformance checkers may report more than one parse error condition if more than one parse error condition exists in the document. Conformance checkers are not required to recover from parse errors, but if they do, they must recover in the same way as user agents.\nL203: \nL204: Tests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/)\nciteturn1188view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/\",\"pattern\":\"32\"}); Total lines: 2959\nL1611: In some contexts, additional cite776†<calc-keyword> values can be defined to be valid. (For example, in cite773†relative color syntax, appropriate channel keywords are allowed.)\nL1612: \nL1613: In addition, cite777†whitespace is required on both sides of the + and - operators. (The * and / operators can be used without white space around them.)\nL1614: \nL1615: Several of the math functions above have additional constraints on what their cite714†<calc-sum> arguments can contain. Check the definitions of the individual functions for details.\nL1616: UAs must support cite715†calculations of at least 32 cite775†<calc-value> terms and at least 32 levels of nesting (parentheses and/or functions). For functions that support an arbitrary number of arguments (such as cite730†min() ), it must also support at least 32 arguments. If a calculation contains more than the supported number of terms, arguments, or nesting it must be treated as if it were invalid.\nL1617: ### 10.9. Type Checking\nL1618: \nL1619: A cite181†math function can be many possible types, such as cite127†<length> , cite279†<number> , etc., depending on the cite715†calculations it contains, as defined below. It can be used anywhere a value of that type is allowed.\nL1620: \nL1621: For example, the cite347†width property accepts cite127†<length> values, so a cite181†math function that resolves to a <length>, such as calc(5px + 1em), can be used in width.\nL1622: Additionally, cite181†math functions that resolve to cite279†<number> can be used in any place that only accepts cite147†<integer> ; the value is cite306†rounded to the nearest integer as it resolves.\nL1623: \nL1624: Operators form sub-expressions, which gain types based on their arguments.\nL2211:   * Defined minimum number of cite149†calc() terms, arguments, and nesting as 32. (cite911†Issue 3462†github.com )\nL2212:   * Defined that mod(-0, infinity) returns cite770†NaN . (cite912†Issue 4723†github.com )\nL2213:   * Deferred cite913†toggle()†www.w3.org and cite914†attr() to Level 5.\nL2214: \nL2215: Changes since cite915†30 September 2021 WD†www.